-- Q4647: Specific Areas and Transactions [medium/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Require the entity to eliminate the related party transaction entirely","Obtain sufficient appropriate evidence about proper accounting and disclosure","Ignore the transaction if the dollar amount is below planning materiality","Report the transaction directly to the Securities and Exchange Commission"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Under AU-C 550, the auditor should obtain sufficient appropriate audit evidence about related party transactions to determine whether they have been properly identified, accounted for, and disclosed per the applicable framework. Understanding the nature and business purpose of these transactions is essential.
Wrong (A): The auditor has no authority to require elimination of a legitimate transaction; the concern is proper accounting and disclosure.
Wrong (C): Even immaterial related party transactions may warrant disclosure and cannot simply be ignored.
Wrong (D): Reporting directly to the SEC is not the auditor''s standard response to identifying a related party transaction.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 4647;

-- Q4651: Specific Areas and Transactions [hard/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Issue an unmodified opinion on the financial statements","Issue an adverse opinion due to inappropriate going concern basis","Issue a qualified opinion with an emphasis-of-matter paragraph","Issue a disclaimer of opinion citing insufficient evidence"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Per AU-C 570, if the entity cannot continue as a going concern and the financial statements are prepared on a going concern basis, that basis is inappropriate. The resulting material misstatement warrants an adverse opinion because the financial statements do not properly reflect the entity''s situation.
Wrong (A): An unmodified opinion is inappropriate when the going concern basis is clearly wrong.
Wrong (C): A qualified opinion is insufficient when the misstatement from an inappropriate basis is pervasive.
Wrong (D): A disclaimer addresses inability to obtain evidence, not a known inappropriate accounting basis.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 4651;

-- Q4653: Specific Areas and Transactions [medium/L3]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["The transaction may not reflect economic reality and could manipulate results","All related party transactions are inherently fraudulent under audit standards","Related parties are prohibited from entering into transactions with each other","The transaction will automatically result in a material misstatement of assets"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): Under AU-C 550, related party transactions at non-arm''s-length terms are concerning because they may not reflect economic substance and could be structured to manipulate financial results. The auditor must evaluate whether such transactions are properly disclosed and accounted for.
Wrong (B): Related party transactions are not inherently fraudulent; they are permitted but require heightened scrutiny.
Wrong (C): No standard prohibits related parties from transacting; the concern is about terms and disclosure.
Wrong (D): Non-arm''s-length terms do not automatically cause a material misstatement; the impact depends on magnitude and disclosure.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 4653;

-- Q4654: Specific Areas and Transactions [hard/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Request management to extend the assessment to at least twelve months","Accept the shorter assessment period without further comment or action","Issue a disclaimer of opinion due to the inadequate assessment period","Modify the evaluation period to a six-month forward-looking window"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): Per AU-C 570 and ASC 205-40, management must evaluate whether substantial doubt exists about the entity''s ability to continue as a going concern for at least one year from the financial statement issuance date. If the assessment covers less, the auditor should request management to extend it.
Wrong (B): Accepting a shortened period without comment fails to meet the auditor''s responsibility under the standard.
Wrong (C): A disclaimer addresses inability to obtain evidence, not an inadequate management assessment that can be corrected.
Wrong (D): Six months is shorter than the required twelve-month period and would not satisfy the standard.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 4654;

-- Q4655: Specific Areas and Transactions [easy/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Evaluate management''s methods, assumptions, and data for appropriateness and reasonableness","Focus only on verifying the mathematical accuracy of the calculations","Accept the estimates without testing because they inherently involve uncertainty","Only test estimates that exceed a predetermined materiality dollar threshold"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): Under AU-C 540, the auditor must evaluate whether management''s methods are appropriate, whether significant assumptions are reasonable and supported by evidence, and whether the underlying data is relevant and reliable. The auditor should also consider management bias.
Wrong (B): Mathematical accuracy is only one element; the auditor must also assess the reasonableness of assumptions and methods.
Wrong (C): Uncertainty does not excuse the auditor from testing; estimates require robust evaluation precisely because of inherent uncertainty.
Wrong (D): All material estimates should be evaluated regardless of whether they exceed a specific threshold.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 4655;

-- Q4657: Specific Areas and Transactions [medium/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Reviewing large or unusual transactions near year-end for undisclosed relationships","Performing a physical observation of the entity''s warehouse inventory","Testing the accuracy of depreciation calculations on fixed assets","Confirming cash balances held at the entity''s primary financial institutions"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): Per AU-C 550, reviewing large or unusual transactions -- particularly those near year-end, with unusual terms, or lacking apparent business rationale -- is an effective procedure to identify previously undisclosed related parties. Such transactions may indicate hidden relationships with management.
Wrong (B): Physical inventory observation tests existence and condition of inventory, not related party relationships.
Wrong (C): Depreciation testing addresses valuation of fixed assets, not potential undisclosed relationships.
Wrong (D): Bank confirmations verify cash balances and are not designed to detect undisclosed related parties.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 4657;

-- Q4658: Specific Areas and Transactions [hard/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Replace the opinion paragraph entirely with going concern language","Follow the opinion paragraph and reference substantial doubt about going concern","Be placed before the opinion and reference the specific financial statement note","Not reference any specific note or disclosure in the financial statements"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (C): Per AU-C 570, when there is substantial doubt about going concern with adequate disclosure, the emphasis-of-matter paragraph should be placed in a separate section and include specific reference to the financial statement note discussing the going concern uncertainty. This placement highlights the matter for report users.
Wrong (A): The emphasis-of-matter paragraph supplements the opinion paragraph; it does not replace it.
Wrong (B): Per AU-C 706, emphasis-of-matter paragraphs are placed after the basis for opinion but before the opinion paragraph for going concern matters.
Wrong (D): The paragraph must reference the specific note in the financial statements that discusses the going concern matter.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 4658;

-- Q4659: Specific Areas and Transactions [easy/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["They are always illegal under federal and state business transaction laws","They always result in penalties under the Internal Revenue Code provisions","They may not be at arm''s length and could distort financial reporting results","They require footnote disclosure only without any financial statement adjustment"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (C): Under AU-C 550, the primary audit risk with related party transactions is that they may not be conducted at arm''s length, meaning terms may not reflect fair market conditions. This creates a risk that financial statements do not faithfully represent the underlying economics of the entity.
Wrong (A): Related party transactions are not inherently illegal; they are permitted but require proper disclosure and scrutiny.
Wrong (B): Related party transactions do not automatically trigger tax penalties; the concern is financial reporting accuracy.
Wrong (D): Depending on the nature and terms, related party transactions may require both disclosure and adjustments to financial statement amounts.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 4659;

-- Q4660: Specific Areas and Transactions [hard/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Level 3 inputs are observable and can be easily verified by the auditor","Level 3 fair value measurements are always immaterial to financial statements","Level 3 inputs are unobservable, creating high estimation uncertainty and subjectivity","Level 3 is the most common category used in fair value measurement reporting"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (C): Per AU-C 540 and ASC 820, Level 3 fair value measurements use significant unobservable inputs that rely heavily on management''s own assumptions and models. These inputs are highly subjective, difficult to verify independently, and susceptible to management bias, creating the greatest audit challenge.
Wrong (A): Level 3 inputs are specifically unobservable; observable inputs characterize Level 1 and Level 2 measurements.
Wrong (B): Level 3 measurements can be highly material, particularly for complex financial instruments and intangible assets.
Wrong (D): Level 3 is the least common category; most fair value measurements use observable Level 1 or Level 2 inputs.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 4660;

-- Q4662: Specific Areas and Transactions [easy/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Transactions that follow standard and commonly accepted industry practices","Transactions with clear business rationale conducted at standard market terms","Transactions that appear to lack substance or involve undisclosed related parties","Transactions that involve only immaterial dollar amounts on the balance sheet"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (C): Per AU-C 240 and AU-C 550, significant unusual transactions that appear to lack business substance, involve previously unidentified related parties, or have been processed outside normal channels are potential fraud indicators. The auditor should investigate the rationale and evaluate the terms.
Wrong (A): Standard industry transactions with clear rationale do not typically raise red flags for fraud risk.
Wrong (B): Transactions with clear business purpose and market terms are routine and do not warrant heightened alertness.
Wrong (D): Immaterial transactions are generally lower risk; the auditor''s concern is with transactions that are both significant and unusual.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 4662;

-- Q4663: Specific Areas and Transactions [hard/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Is expected in normal operations and does not affect audit procedures","Only requires documentation if the bias exceeds ten percent of the estimate","Automatically requires the auditor to issue an adverse opinion on the statements","Should be evaluated for cumulative effect that may cause material misstatement"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (D): Under AU-C 540, management bias does not automatically result in a material misstatement, but the auditor must evaluate the cumulative effect across all estimates. If the cumulative bias results in material misstatement or indicates possible intent to mislead, the auditor must take appropriate action.
Wrong (A): Consistent directional bias is not expected and should always concern the auditor as a potential fraud risk indicator.
Wrong (B): No ten percent threshold exists in the standards; the auditor evaluates bias qualitatively and quantitatively regardless of magnitude.
Wrong (C): An adverse opinion is not automatically required; the response depends on whether the cumulative bias actually causes material misstatement.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 4663;

-- Q4667: Modifications to the Auditor Report [easy/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["The financial statements contain only immaterial misstatements overall","The auditor cannot obtain sufficient evidence and possible effects are pervasive","The auditor discovers a confirmed instance of fraud during the engagement","Management disagrees with the auditor''s proposed accounting treatment changes"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Per AU-C 705, a disclaimer of opinion is expressed when the auditor is unable to obtain sufficient appropriate audit evidence on which to base an opinion and concludes that the possible effects of undetected misstatements could be both material and pervasive.
Wrong (A): If misstatements are only immaterial, an unmodified opinion is appropriate with no modification needed.
Wrong (C): Discovery of fraud does not automatically trigger a disclaimer; the response depends on the nature and pervasiveness of the effect.
Wrong (D): Disagreement with management over accounting treatment is a misstatement issue, potentially leading to a qualified or adverse opinion, not a disclaimer.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 4667;

-- Q4668: Modifications to the Auditor Report [medium/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Modify the auditor''s opinion on the fairness of the financial statements","Highlight a properly disclosed matter that is fundamental to users'' understanding","Replace the basis for opinion paragraph with additional explanatory language","Limit the auditor''s legal liability for the matters discussed in the report"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Per AU-C 706, an emphasis-of-matter paragraph draws attention to a matter that is appropriately presented or disclosed in the financial statements and is of such importance that it is fundamental to users'' understanding. Examples include going concern uncertainties and significant related party transactions.
Wrong (A): An emphasis-of-matter paragraph does not modify the auditor''s opinion; the opinion remains unmodified.
Wrong (C): The emphasis-of-matter paragraph is a separate section and does not replace the basis for opinion paragraph.
Wrong (D): The paragraph''s purpose is informational transparency, not liability limitation for the auditor.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 4668;

-- Q4669: Modifications to the Auditor Report [medium/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Always modifies the audit opinion expressed on the financial statements","Addresses matters not in the financial statements but relevant to the audit","Is used exclusively for going concern uncertainties and no other purposes","Replaces the opinion paragraph with alternative explanatory language entirely"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Per AU-C 706, an other-matter paragraph addresses matters not presented or disclosed in the financial statements that are relevant to users'' understanding of the audit, the auditor''s responsibilities, or the auditor''s report. Examples include references to prior-period auditors or restrictions on report distribution.
Wrong (A): Neither an other-matter nor an emphasis-of-matter paragraph modifies the audit opinion.
Wrong (C): Going concern issues are addressed by emphasis-of-matter paragraphs, not other-matter paragraphs.
Wrong (D): The other-matter paragraph is a separate additional section; it does not replace the opinion paragraph.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 4669;

-- Q4671: Modifications to the Auditor Report [medium/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Only the standard unmodified opinion language without additional paragraphs","A basis for qualified opinion paragraph describing the misstatement and its effects","A reference to the client''s external legal counsel''s opinion letter","An emphasis-of-matter paragraph instead of a basis for opinion paragraph"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Per AU-C 705, a qualified opinion due to a framework departure requires a basis for qualified opinion paragraph that describes the nature of the misstatement, quantifies its effect if practicable, and explains why the departure is material. The opinion paragraph then uses "except for" language.
Wrong (A): Standard unmodified language is inappropriate when the auditor has identified a material departure from the framework.
Wrong (C): External legal counsel''s letter is not a required element of a qualified opinion report.
Wrong (D): A known departure requires a basis for qualified opinion paragraph, not an emphasis-of-matter paragraph which is for properly disclosed matters.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 4671;

-- Q4672: Modifications to the Auditor Report [medium/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["An unmodified opinion since alternative procedures were attempted","An adverse opinion because the financial statements are materially misstated","A disclaimer of opinion due to inability to form any conclusion","A qualified opinion due to a scope limitation on audit evidence"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (D): Per AU-C 705, when the auditor cannot obtain sufficient appropriate evidence due to a scope limitation and the possible effects are material but not pervasive, a qualified opinion is appropriate. The basis for qualified opinion paragraph should describe the scope limitation and its potential effect.
Wrong (A): An unmodified opinion requires sufficient appropriate evidence, which is unavailable due to the scope limitation.
Wrong (B): An adverse opinion addresses known material misstatements, not inability to obtain evidence.
Wrong (C): A disclaimer is appropriate only when possible effects are both material and pervasive, not merely material.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 4672;

-- Q4673: Modifications to the Auditor Report [hard/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Scope limitations prevent obtaining evidence for multiple significant accounts","The entity changed its depreciation method from straight-line to accelerated","The entity adopted a new accounting standard changing revenue presentation","The auditor disagrees with management''s allowance for doubtful accounts estimate"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): Per AU-C 705, a disclaimer is appropriate when scope limitations are so severe and pervasive that the auditor cannot obtain sufficient evidence on which to base an opinion. Restrictions affecting multiple significant accounts representing a substantial portion of the financial statements are both material and pervasive.
Wrong (B): A change in depreciation method is an accounting change, not a scope limitation; it may require an emphasis-of-matter paragraph.
Wrong (C): Adopting a new standard affects presentation but does not prevent the auditor from obtaining evidence.
Wrong (D): A disagreement over an estimate is a potential misstatement issue leading to a qualified or adverse opinion, not a disclaimer.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 4673;

-- Q4674: Modifications to the Auditor Report [easy/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Except for the effects of the matter described, the statements present fairly","In our opinion, the financial statements present fairly in all material respects","We do not express an opinion on the accompanying financial statements","In our opinion, the financial statements do not present fairly overall"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): Per AU-C 705, a qualified opinion uses "except for" language referencing the Basis for Qualified Opinion section. This clearly communicates that the qualification is limited to a specific identified matter while the remainder of the statements are fairly stated.
Wrong (B): This is the language for an unmodified opinion, which is inappropriate when a material GAAP departure exists.
Wrong (C): This is disclaimer language used when the auditor cannot obtain sufficient evidence, not when a specific departure is identified.
Wrong (D): This is adverse opinion language used when the misstatement is both material and pervasive.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 4674;

-- Q4676: Modifications to the Auditor Report [hard/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["The extent to which misstatements affect or could affect the financial statements overall","Whether the misstatement is large in dollar amount relative to total assets","Whether management agrees with the auditor''s assessment of the misstatement","The total number of audit procedures performed during the engagement"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): Per AU-C 705, pervasiveness describes how widely misstatements spread across the financial statements. A misstatement is pervasive if it is not confined to specific elements, represents a substantial proportion of the statements, or fundamentally undermines users'' understanding of disclosures.
Wrong (B): Dollar amount alone does not determine pervasiveness; the key factor is how broadly the misstatement affects the financial statements.
Wrong (C): Management''s agreement or disagreement is irrelevant to whether the effects of a misstatement are pervasive.
Wrong (D): The number of procedures performed has no bearing on the concept of pervasiveness as defined in auditing standards.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 4676;

-- Q4678: Modifications to the Auditor Report [hard/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["An unmodified opinion because management was not at fault for the limitation","An adverse opinion because the underlying accounting records were destroyed","A qualified opinion or disclaimer depending on materiality and pervasiveness","No effect on the auditor''s report since the limitation was circumstantial"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (C): Per AU-C 705, a scope limitation -- regardless of whether imposed by management or by circumstances -- requires the auditor to evaluate the potential effects. If possible effects are material but not pervasive, a qualified opinion is appropriate; if material and pervasive, a disclaimer is warranted.
Wrong (A): The cause of the limitation does not excuse the auditor from evaluating its effect on the financial statements.
Wrong (B): An adverse opinion addresses known misstatements, not inability to obtain evidence due to destroyed records.
Wrong (D): Any scope limitation that could have material effects requires modification of the auditor''s report.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 4678;

-- Q4680: Modifications to the Auditor Report [hard/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Issue an unmodified opinion because contingent liabilities are inherently uncertain","Issue a disclaimer of opinion due to insufficient information about the liability","Issue a qualified opinion with omitted disclosure in the basis paragraph","Issue an unmodified opinion with an emphasis-of-matter paragraph added"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (C): Per AU-C 705, when required disclosures are omitted and the omission is material but not pervasive, the auditor should issue a qualified opinion. The basis for qualified opinion paragraph should describe the omission and, to the extent practicable, include the missing information.
Wrong (A): Uncertainty does not excuse the omission of a required disclosure; the departure from the framework warrants modification.
Wrong (B): A disclaimer addresses inability to obtain evidence, not a known omission of required disclosures.
Wrong (D): An emphasis-of-matter paragraph is for properly disclosed matters; a missing required disclosure requires opinion modification.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 4680;

-- Q4681: Modifications to the Auditor Report [easy/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Describe the specific audit procedures performed during the engagement","Describe management''s responsibilities for preparing the financial statements","List all individual misstatements the auditor found during audit fieldwork","Provide the foundation for the opinion including the auditor''s responsibilities"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (D): Per AU-C 700, the basis for opinion section states the audit was conducted in accordance with GAAS, references the auditor''s independence and ethical responsibilities, and confirms that evidence obtained is sufficient. For modified opinions, it describes the specific reasons for the modification.
Wrong (A): The section references GAAS compliance broadly but does not describe individual audit procedures performed.
Wrong (B): Management''s responsibilities are covered in a separate section of the auditor''s report.
Wrong (C): The auditor''s report does not list individual misstatements; it addresses only those affecting the opinion.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 4681;

-- Q4682: Modifications to the Auditor Report [hard/L4]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["An adverse opinion on all of the entity''s financial statements","An unmodified opinion on all of the entity''s financial statements","A disclaimer of opinion on all of the entity''s financial statements","A qualified opinion on the affected statement with unmodified on the others"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (D): Per AU-C 705, when a material misstatement affects only one financial statement and does not pervasively affect the others, the auditor may issue a qualified or adverse opinion on the affected statement while issuing an unmodified opinion on the remaining financial statements.
Wrong (A): An adverse opinion on all statements is excessive when the misstatement is confined to one statement and is not pervasive.
Wrong (B): An unmodified opinion on all statements ignores the known material misstatement in the affected statement.
Wrong (C): A disclaimer addresses inability to obtain evidence, not a known misstatement limited to one financial statement.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 4682;

-- Q4683: Modifications to the Auditor Report [medium/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Going concern uncertainty about the entity''s ability to continue operations","A voluntary change in accounting principle adopted during the current period","A significant subsequent event that was appropriately disclosed in the notes","A known departure from the applicable financial reporting framework used"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (D): Per AU-C 706, an emphasis-of-matter paragraph highlights matters that are appropriately presented or disclosed in the financial statements. It applies to going concern, changes in accounting principles, and significant subsequent events. A known framework departure requires a qualified or adverse opinion, not an emphasis-of-matter paragraph.
Wrong (A): Going concern uncertainty, when properly disclosed, is a common and appropriate use of an emphasis-of-matter paragraph.
Wrong (B): A change in accounting principle, when properly disclosed, is an appropriate subject for an emphasis-of-matter paragraph.
Wrong (C): A significant subsequent event that is appropriately disclosed may warrant an emphasis-of-matter paragraph.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 4683;

-- Q4684: Modifications to the Auditor Report [hard/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Include the standard description of auditor responsibility and evidence obtained","Include an emphasis-of-matter paragraph highlighting the scope limitation","Be identical to an unmodified report except for changes in the opinion paragraph","Omit the reasonable assurance statement and describe the reasons for disclaimer"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (D): Per AU-C 705, when disclaiming an opinion, the report should state that the auditor does not express an opinion. It must omit the standard statements about obtaining reasonable assurance and sufficient appropriate evidence. The basis for disclaimer paragraph should clearly describe the reasons.
Wrong (A): Including the standard responsibility statement would be misleading because the auditor is not providing assurance.
Wrong (B): An emphasis-of-matter paragraph is inappropriate in a disclaimer report; the basis for disclaimer paragraph serves the explanatory function.
Wrong (C): A disclaimer report differs substantially from an unmodified report in both the opinion and responsibility sections.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 4684;

-- Q4685: Other Information and Supplementary Information [easy/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["The audited financial statements and all related footnote disclosures","Financial and non-financial information in documents with audited statements","Only the management discussion and analysis section of the annual report","Supplementary schedules that are audited together with the financial statements"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Per AU-C 720, other information refers to financial and non-financial information (other than the audited financial statements and the auditor''s report) included in a document containing audited financial statements. Examples include CEO letters, statistical summaries, and management discussion.
Wrong (A): The audited financial statements themselves are not "other information"; they are the subject of the audit opinion.
Wrong (C): MD&A is only one example; other information encompasses all non-audited content in the document.
Wrong (D): Supplementary schedules that are audited are covered under AU-C 725, not the "other information" definition.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 4685;

-- Q4686: Other Information and Supplementary Information [easy/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Audit the other information to the same extent as the financial statements","Read it and consider whether it is materially inconsistent with the statements","Ignore the other information entirely because it falls outside the audit scope","Provide a separate audit opinion on the other information in the report"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Per AU-C 720, the auditor must read the other information and consider whether it is materially inconsistent with the audited financial statements or the auditor''s knowledge obtained during the audit. The auditor must remain alert to material inconsistencies or misstatements of fact.
Wrong (A): The auditor does not audit the other information; the responsibility is limited to reading and considering consistency.
Wrong (C): Ignoring the other information would violate AU-C 720, which imposes a read-and-consider responsibility.
Wrong (D): The auditor does not express a separate opinion on other information under AU-C 720.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 4686;

-- Q4687: Other Information and Supplementary Information [medium/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Is required by the framework to accompany the basic financial statements","Is always audited along with the financial statements by the engagement team","Is optional and may be omitted without any departure from the framework","Is prepared by the auditor rather than by management of the entity"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): Per AU-C 730, required supplementary information (RSI) is information that a designated standards setter such as GASB or FASB requires to accompany the basic financial statements. While RSI is not part of the basic statements, its omission constitutes a departure from prescribed guidelines.
Wrong (B): RSI is not audited; the auditor applies limited procedures such as inquiries and comparison to the financial statements.
Wrong (C): RSI is not optional; omitting it is a departure from the applicable framework''s requirements.
Wrong (D): RSI is prepared by management, not the auditor; the auditor''s role is to apply limited procedures to it.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 4687;

-- Q4688: Other Information and Supplementary Information [medium/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Ignore the inconsistency because the other information is not subject to audit","Determine whether the statements or other information needs revision","Immediately issue a qualified opinion on the audited financial statements","Report the inconsistency only to the Securities and Exchange Commission"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Per AU-C 720, when a material inconsistency is identified, the auditor should determine whether the audited financial statements or the other information needs revision and discuss the matter with management. If the financial statements need revision, this affects the audit opinion.
Wrong (A): Ignoring a known material inconsistency would violate the auditor''s responsibility under AU-C 720.
Wrong (C): A qualified opinion is not automatically appropriate; the auditor must first determine the source of the inconsistency.
Wrong (D): Reporting only to the SEC is not the standard response; the auditor first discusses the matter with management.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 4688;

-- Q4689: Other Information and Supplementary Information [medium/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Required supplementary information mandated by the standards setter","Other information as defined by AU-C 720 for annual report content","Supplementary information accompanying the audited financial statements","Additional voluntary disclosures not addressed by auditing standards"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (C): Per AU-C 725, supplementary information accompanying the audited financial statements refers to information presented outside the basic financial statements that is not required by the framework but that management chooses to present alongside the audited statements. The auditor may report on this information in relation to the statements as a whole.
Wrong (A): Required supplementary information (RSI) under AU-C 730 is mandated by the standards setter, which is a different category.
Wrong (B): Other information under AU-C 720 covers non-audited content in documents containing audited statements, a broader category.
Wrong (D): This information is specifically addressed by AU-C 725 and is a recognized category in auditing standards.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 4689;
